d, plus the savings and other near-money a country's central bank counts; narrow money is the part that can be spent at once, mainly cash and current accounts. This book splits the broad money figure into narrow money and the rest, in the country's own currency, as the OECD collects them each month from the central bank, not seasonally adjusted. Countries draw these lines differently, so amounts are never added across countries or turned into one currency here, and a change in a country's own definition shows as a jump between the two rows without any money moving. Source: OECD."}